    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In 48 a confederation of eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> tribes, with 40,000 to 50,000 men, seceded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> Punu and acclaimed Bi as chanyu, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a>.</li><li>From 50 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> were tributaries of Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, resettled in eight frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> beside Chinese settlers and reliant on Han trade and annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>.</li><li>They served as Han auxiliaries against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a>, but after rebellions in the 180s the group dissolved, and in 216 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> detained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huchuquan">Huchuquan</a> at Ye.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Han court often installed southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyus</a> who were partial to its interests, and in the late 2nd century the chanyu's own followers killed him for leading them into the Han's internal wars.</p></aside>
    </section>

