    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modu-chanyu">Modu Chanyu</a> after 209 BCE. In the civil war of 60–53 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a> moved south and submitted to the Han, using Chinese support against his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhizhi">Zhizhi</a>; the Han reduced the Xiongnu from a "brotherly state" to an "outer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>". <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">Tributary relations</a> lapsed under Huduershi (18–48 CE), who took advantage of the upheavals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xin-dynasty">Xin dynasty</a> to regain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a> and at the height of his power compared himself to Modu.</p><p>Growing regionalism undid that recovery. Against the principle of fraternal succession set by Huhanye, Huduershi named his son Punu heir. Bi (Pi), the Rizhu King of the Right and eldest son of the preceding chanyu, had the stronger claim and refused to attend the annual meeting at the chanyu's court; in 46 Punu took the throne nonetheless. In 48 a confederation of eight Xiongnu tribes in Bi's power base in the south, with a force of 40,000 to 50,000 men, seceded from Punu and acclaimed Bi as chanyu. This kingdom became known as the Southern Xiongnu, while Punu's rump state around the Orkhon, in north central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> were plagued by natural disasters and misfortunes from the start, and in 50 they submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary relations</a> with Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Their people were resettled in eight frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, and large numbers of Chinese were settled there at the same time, in mixed Han–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> settlements. Economically the Southern Xiongnu came to rely on trade with the Han and on annual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> from the Chinese court. The poor climate and living conditions of the frontier weighed on them, and many tribesmen left the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> to return to the steppe and join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>.</p><p>The Xiongnu were a herding people; from their flocks and their horse trade with China their diet is thought to have consisted mainly of mutton, horse meat and wild geese. The Book of Jin names nineteen Xiongnu tribes that resettled within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>, among them the Chuge, the Qiangqu, the Helai and the Lijie; the Chuge, also called the Xiuchuge, were said to be the most powerful and prestigious. With the fall of the Southern Xiongnu their descendants intermixed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, Xianbei, Wuhuan and other peoples, and the Xiongnu name gradually disappeared; they were called simply Hu, or formed the core of "mixed Hu" groups.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> served the Han as auxiliaries in defending the northern frontier against nomadic forces. In 49 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> were dealt a heavy defeat by the Southern Xiongnu, and the same year the Han governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, Zhai Tong, enticed the Wuhuan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> to attack the north. The Southern Xiongnu played a role in the defeat of the Northern Xiongnu, who suffered famines from locust plagues, lost their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> Youliu to the Xianbei in 87, were crushed by the Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dou-xian">Dou Xian</a> at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> in 89, and after another Han attack in 91 saw their chanyu flee to the north-west.</p><p>The victory brought no peace. The surrendered Northern Xiongnu rebelled in 94, dissatisfied with a newly appointed chanyu, and acclaimed Fenghou, who led them beyond the border. The southern tribes now bore the brunt of incessant raids by the rising Xianbei. Those who stayed mutinied from time to time, but failed to gain enough support and were easily put down. In the late 2nd century the southern chanyu sent his people against the Yellow Turban Rebellion and then against the Wuhuan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> in 188. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> feared unending military service to the Han court, and a rebellious faction allied with the Xiuchuge, who had killed the provincial inspector of Bingzhou, and killed the chanyu.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> were ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>, with the tuqi kings beneath him; the Tuqi King of the Left was normally the heir presumptive. Below them came paired officials of the left and right, the guli, the army commanders, the great governors, the danghu and the gudu, and beneath them the commanders of a thousand, a hundred and ten men, so that the nation was organised like an army. When a chanyu died, power could pass to his younger brother if his son was not of age; this kept an adult on the throne but invited rival claims in later generations, and it was such a dispute that divided the Xiongnu in 48.</p><p>As tributaries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> came under Han terms first set for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huhanye">Huhanye</a> in 53 BCE: the chanyu or his representatives were to pay homage at the capital, send a hostage prince and present tribute. In 50 the southern chanyu was ordered to set his court in Meiji County, Xihe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a>. The Chinese court interfered in his politics, often installing chanyus partial to Han interests, and his authority waned as tribesmen left for the steppe. After the chanyu was killed in 188 the Han court named his son Yufuluo, entitled Chizhi Shizhu, but the rebels expelled him; they elected a chanyu of their own and, when he died a year later, left the post vacant under an elderly nominal king.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> show the limits of what Barfield calls the "inner frontier strategy". In the first Xiongnu civil war, in 60 BCE, the weaker party moved south, submitted to the dominant Chinese regime and used its overlord's resources to defeat its rivals and re-establish the empire. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> divided again around 47 CE the strategy failed: the southern ruler could not defeat the northern one, and the Xiongnu remained divided. The southern tribes nonetheless helped the Han destroy the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a>, most of whom were absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>.</p><p>Settled inside the frontier, the descendants of the Southern Xiongnu shaped northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> for centuries. In 304 the Five Divisions acclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yuan">Liu Yuan</a>, who claimed descent from the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyus</a>, though more recent studies suggest he was a Chuge who forged his genealogy; he founded the Han-Zhao state, whose forces sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> in 311. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a>, founder of the Later Zhao, came from the Qiangqu tribe of the Southern Xiongnu, and the chieftains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiefu">Tiefu</a>, who founded the Helian Xia, descended from Qubei. By the 7th century the Xiongnu had been completely integrated into Chinese society.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After his expulsion Yufuluo travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> to seek aid, but the Han court was in disorder from the clash between the Grand General He Jin and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> and the intervention of the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-zhuo">Dong Zhuo</a>. He settled with his followers around Pingyang, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fen-river">Fen River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, where he died in 195 and was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huchuquan">Huchuquan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> had effectively dissolved: many of their tribes broke away and kept their distance from the Han civil war, while Yufuluo's group and the Xiuchuge were drawn into it and later subdued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a>.</p><p>The upheaval cost the Han several frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of Bing province, among them Shuofang and Yunzhong, which were lost to hostile tribes; Cao Cao abolished them and reduced Bing to the region of modern Shanxi. In 216 he detained Huchuquan in the city of Ye and sent Huchuquan's uncle, Qubei, to manage the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in Shanxi. Huchuquan held the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a> in name until his death, after which it was abolished. Brought closer to Chinese rule, the tribes could be watched for rebellion and used as cavalry auxiliaries; they were later divided into the Five Divisions around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> Commandery, each led by a local chief under the eye of a Chinese resident.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> were the eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> tribes that broke away under Bi in 48 and became tributaries of the Han, resettled along the northern frontier and paid to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a>. Weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> raids, Han interference and rebellion, they dissolved in the 190s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> ended their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanyu">chanyu</a>'s court in 216.</em></p>

